<Guide
  title="Our Common Bond Part 3: Government and the Law"
  description="Key facts from Part 3 in plain English with booklet page numbers."
  path="australia/our-common-bond/part-3/"
  h1="Part 3: Government and the law in Australia"
  answer="Part 3 of Our Common Bond (pages 23 to 32) explains how you have your say, how the system of government was set up, who holds power, how laws are made and enforced, and some important laws. Here are its key facts in plain English, with the booklet page for each."
  sources={['booklet', 'prepare']}
  related={[GUIDES.values, GUIDES.part2, GUIDES.ocb]}
  kind="booklet"
>
  <p>This is a summary to help you study, not a replacement for the booklet. Read the <a href={SOURCES.booklet}>full booklet from Home Affairs</a> too. At the citizenship ceremony you pledge to uphold and obey the laws of Australia.</p>

  <h2 id="your-say">How you have your say <span class="pg">page 24</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Citizens aged 18 or over must enrol and vote in federal, state and territory elections and referendums. If you are not correctly enrolled, you can’t vote. Not voting without a good reason can mean a fine.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Australian Electoral Commission (AEC)</strong> runs federal elections and referendums and keeps the electoral roll. It is independent of the government.</li>
    <li>Voting is by <strong>secret ballot</strong>: no one may know whom you voted for unless you tell them.</li>
    <li>You can contact your elected representative to raise concerns about government policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="federation">Federation and the Constitution <span class="pg">page 25</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Before 1901 there were six separate, self-governing British colonies. On <strong>1 January 1901</strong> they united as the <strong>Commonwealth of Australia</strong>.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Australian Constitution</strong> sets out the basic rules for the government of Australia. It set up the Parliament (the House of Representatives and the Senate) and the <strong>High Court</strong>, which has the ultimate power to apply and interpret the laws.</li>
    <li>The Constitution can only be changed by a <strong>referendum</strong>, which needs a <strong>double majority</strong>: a majority of voters in a majority of states, and a majority of voters across the nation. In the <strong>1967 Referendum</strong>, more than 90 per cent voted ‘Yes’ to count Aboriginal peoples in the Census.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="separation-of-powers">How the power of government is divided <span class="pg">page 26</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Legislative power</strong> (Parliament): making and changing laws.</li>
    <li><strong>Executive power</strong> (the Prime Minister, ministers and the Governor-General): putting laws into practice.</li>
    <li><strong>Judicial power</strong> (judges): interpreting and applying the law. Courts are independent of parliament and government.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Dividing power this way stops one person or group from holding all the power.</p>

  <h2 id="head-of-state">Head of State and the Governor-General <span class="pg">pages 26 to 27</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Australia is a <strong>constitutional monarchy</strong>. The Head of State is the King of Australia, <strong>His Majesty King Charles III</strong>, who must act in line with the Constitution and has no day-to-day role in government.</li>
    <li>The King appoints the <strong>Governor-General</strong> as his representative, on the Prime Minister’s advice. In each state, a <strong>Governor</strong> represents the King.</li>
    <li>The Governor-General is neutral. Their role includes signing Bills into law (<strong>Royal Assent</strong>), ceremonial duties, approving the appointment of the government and ministers, federal judges and others, starting the process for a federal election, and acting as Commander-in-Chief of the Defence Force.</li>
    <li>The leader of the Australian Government is the <strong>Prime Minister</strong>; a state government’s leader is the <strong>Premier</strong>; a territory government’s leader is the <strong>Chief Minister</strong>; a local government’s leader is the <strong>Mayor</strong> or <strong>Shire President</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="parliament">The Australian Parliament <span class="pg">pages 27 to 28</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>House of Representatives</strong> (the Lower House or People’s House) has over 150 members, each representing an electorate. The number for each state and territory depends on its population.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Senate</strong> (the Upper House, House of Review or States’ House) has <strong>76 senators</strong>: 12 from each state and 2 each from the ACT and the Northern Territory. All states have equal representation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="three-levels">Three levels of government <span class="pg">pages 28 to 29</span></h2>
  <table class="plain">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">What each level of government is responsible for</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Examples of what it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Australian Government</th><td>Taxation, immigration and citizenship, defence, social security, foreign affairs, postal services, airports and air safety, trade</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">State and territory</th><td>Hospitals and health, schools, roads and railways, police and ambulance, public transport, forestry</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Local councils</th><td>Local roads and footpaths, parks and pools, rubbish collection, libraries, building permits, animal and noise control</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>States have rights recognised by the Constitution; territories do not, and the Australian Government can change self-governing territory laws.</p>

  <h2 id="government-formed">Parties and how a government is formed <span class="pg">page 30</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The main political parties are the Liberal Party of Australia, the Australian Labor Party, the Nationals and the Australian Greens. Members who belong to no party are ‘independents’.</li>
    <li>After a federal election, the party or coalition with the majority in the House of Representatives forms the government; its leader becomes Prime Minister. The second largest forms the Opposition.</li>
    <li>Ministers are responsible for areas called portfolios. The most senior ministers make up the <strong>Cabinet</strong>, the key decision-making body.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="laws-made">How laws are made <span class="pg">page 30</span></h2>
  <p>A proposed law is a <strong>Bill</strong>. Both Houses consider, debate and vote on it. If a majority in each House agrees, it goes to the Governor-General, whose signature (Royal Assent) makes it law.</p>

  <h2 id="laws-enforced">How laws are enforced <span class="pg">page 31</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Courts are independent. Judges and magistrates are the highest authority in a court, and the government cannot remove them for disagreeing with their decisions.</li>
    <li>People are <strong>innocent until found guilty</strong> in a court, and everyone has the right to a lawyer. A jury of ordinary citizens decides guilt in some cases; the judge decides the penalty.</li>
    <li>The police are independent of the government. States and the Northern Territory have their own police; the <strong>Australian Federal Police</strong> investigates crimes against federal laws and polices the ACT. It is a serious crime to bribe a police officer, or even to offer a bribe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="important-laws">Important laws <span class="pg">page 32</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not knowing a law is no excuse.</li>
    <li>Serious crimes include murder, assault, sexual assault, violence against people or property, armed robbery or theft, sexual relations with children under the age of consent, dangerous driving, possession of illegal drugs, and fraud.</li>
    <li><strong>Domestic and family violence</strong> is against the law, including threats and controlling behaviour. If you or someone you know is in danger, contact the police.</li>
    <li>To drive you need a local driver’s licence and a registered car. Everyone must wear a seatbelt; babies and young children need an approved car seat. Driving after drugs, over the alcohol limit, or while using a hand-held mobile device is illegal.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Pledge’s free practice questions cover Part 1 and the values questions. For the whole booklet, the <a href={SOURCES.practice}>Home Affairs practice test</a> is free, and the <a href={url(PRACTICE.path)}>Pledge practice test</a> is marked with both pass rules.</p>
</Guide>
